n\nBut implementing parallel merge correctly is tricky due to exact tie-breaking. We need ensure final selected set exactly matches sequential algorithm. Let's define ordering: For any two points a,b with distances da,db and indices ia,ib (0-based), point a is \"better\" than b if da < db or (da == db && ia < ib). The top-k are the k best according to this total order. Sequential heap maintains that.\n\nIf we split into T contiguous chunks: chunk t covers indices [start_t, end_t). Within each chunk, because indices increase, the local ordering is consistent with global for points within same chunk (since if da==db and ia<ib both in chunk, then ia<ib automatically; but also across chunks? For merging we need compare by distance first, then index. So we can maintain per-chunk top-k heaps using same comparator on (distance,index). Then merge T sorted lists of size <=k into global top-k. Since each list is already the best k from that chunk according to total order restricted to chunk, and chunks partition all points, the union of these lists contains at least the global top-k? Actually if a point is not in its chunk's local top-k, then there are at least k points in that chunk better than it; globally those k points are also better (since same comparator), so it cannot be in global top-k. Thus global top-k subset of union of local top-k lists. So merging the T*K candidates yields correct result.\n\nWe can implement this with OpenMP: each thread processes a contiguous slice, builds its own heap arrays (size k). Then merge sequentially or parallel? Merging T heaps of size <=k is O(T*k log(T*k)) if we use another heap; T=4, k maybe small. Could be done in main thread after parallel region.\n\nBut careful: The current code uses `#pragma omp parallel for simd` on distance loop only. We could combine distance computation and local selection per chunk inside the same parallel region to avoid storing all distances? That would reduce memory traffic. However we need xs, ys arrays already built; we can compute dists locally in each thread without global dists array. But then we don't need dists at all! The current code stores dists for later sequential selection. If we do per-chunk local heaps during distance computation, we avoid storing total distances and also parallelize selection. That could be a significant improvement: no large dists allocation/writes/reads; each thread computes its chunk's distances on the fly and updates its local heap.\n\nBut need to preserve exact tie-breaking across chunks. Since each thread processes contiguous indices in increasing order (if we use schedule(static) with default chunking, OpenMP assigns contiguous blocks? With `schedule(static)` without chunk size, it divides iterations into approximately equal contiguous chunks; yes for parallel for, static gives contiguous chunks by default). So within a thread, p increases. We can maintain local heap using same comparator.\n\nThen after the parallel region, merge T heaps. Need to know number of threads used (OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 per runtime facts). But code should work with any number; we can use omp_get_max_threads() or just allocate arrays for up to OMP_NUM_THREADS? We can dynamically allocate array of heap structs sized by omp_get_num_threads inside parallel region? Better: Use OpenMP worksharing with `#pragma omp parallel` and manually partition loop, each thread has its own local heap. After the parallel region (with implicit barrier), merge.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- In knn(), after building xs, ys, plabels arrays (or maybe we can avoid xs/ys? We need them for distance; but we could also compute distances directly from groups without flattening? Flattening helps SIMD and cache. Keep.)\n- Instead of allocating dists array, allocate an array of local heap structures: one per thread. Each heap has float *d (size k), long *idx, char *lab, int n.\n- Use `#pragma omp parallel` with private variables; each thread gets its tid via omp_get_thread_num(); initialize its heap arrays (malloc inside? Could allocate before parallel for all threads using max threads). Simpler: allocate a 2D array of size num_threads x k. But num_threads unknown until runtime; we can use `omp_get_max_threads()` to allocate.\n- Then each thread processes a contiguous range of p values. We need to partition total among threads manually or use `#pragma omp for schedule(static)` inside parallel region with reduction? But we want local heaps, not shared. Use:\n```\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    // initialize local heap arrays (already allocated)\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static) nowait?\n    for (long p=0; p<(long)total; ++p) {\n        float dx = xs[p]-tx;\n        float dy = ys[p]-ty;\n        float d = dx*dx+dy*dy;\n        // update local heap\n    }\n}\n```\nBut `#pragma omp for` distributes iterations among threads, each thread updates its own heap. Since the loop body has no dependencies across p except through local heap (private to thread), it's fine. The schedule(static) gives contiguous chunks by default? Actually OpenMP static schedule without chunk size divides into equal-sized contiguous chunks in order of iteration space; yes.\n\nAfter parallel region, merge heaps:\n- Create a global max-heap or just collect all candidates from each local heap (up to num_threads*k). Then select top-k using same comparator. Since total candidates small (<=4k), we can simply copy them into arrays and run the existing sequential selection algorithm over that candidate list? But careful: The union of local heaps may contain duplicates? No, each point appears in exactly one chunk, so no duplicate indices. We need to select top-k from these candidates according to comparator. Since each local heap is already sorted as a max-heap but not necessarily fully ordered; we can just push all candidates into a new global heap of size k using the same logic (heap_push/replace). That yields correct result.\n\nBut there's subtlety: The sequential algorithm processes points in increasing p and uses tie-breaking by index. Our per-chunk heaps also process increasing p within chunk, so they maintain local top-k correctly. Merging all candidates with a heap that compares by (distance,index) will yield global top-k because the comparator is total order independent of processing order; any selection algorithm using this comparator on the full set yields same result regardless of insertion order as long as it maintains best k according to comparator. The sequential algorithm's tie-breaking by index is encoded in comparator; so merging with heap over union candidates works.\n\nThus we can parallelize both distance and selection, avoiding dists array entirely. This should improve speed if selection was bottleneck or memory traffic from dists.\n\nNeed ensure correctness for all sizes including total < k? If total <= k, then local heaps may have fewer than k; merging still yields all points sorted by comparator; final labels count works. The current code handles heap_n < k and fills remaining with -1 in labels array before qsort. We need replicate: after merge, if global heap has less than k elements (shouldn't happen because total >=? Could be total < k), then fill rest with -1. But the original sequential algorithm would push all points into heap until full; if total<k, heap_n=total and labels array size k filled with heap_lab for i<heap_n else -1. Our merge should produce same.\n\nAlso need handle case k==0? Probably not allowed (k positive). We can assume.\n\nMemory: allocate local heaps arrays of size num_threads * k each for d, idx, lab. Use `omp_get_max_threads()` to determine max threads; but actual number may be less due to OMP_NUM_THREADS env or dynamic. Better use `omp_get_num_threads()` inside parallel region and allocate per-thread arrays dynamically there? But then merging after parallel needs access to all heaps. We can allocate a global array of pointers sized by max threads, each thread allocates its own heap arrays (size k) using malloc inside parallel; store pointer in global array indexed by tid. After parallel, merge.\n\nSimpler: Since runtime facts say OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 and cpu_cores 4, we can assume at most some number but code should be general. Use `int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();` before parallel; allocate arrays of size max_threads * k for d, idx, lab (flattened). Each thread uses its tid slice.\n\nPseudo:\n```\nint nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();\nfloat *local_d = malloc((size_t)nthreads * k * sizeof(float));\nlong *local_idx = malloc((size_t)nthreads * k * sizeof(long));\nchar *local_lab = malloc((size_t)nthreads * k * sizeof(char));\nint *local_n = calloc(nthreads, sizeof(int));\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)tid*k;\n    long *hi = local_idx + (size_t)tid*k;\n    char *hl = local_lab + (size_t)tid*k;\n    int hn = 0;\n\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p=0; p<(long)total; ++p) {\n        float dx = xs[p]-tx;\n        float dy = ys[p]-ty;\n        float d = dx*dx+dy*dy;\n        if (hn < k) {\n            heap_push(hd, hi, hl, &hn, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        } else if (is_worse(hd[0], hi[0], d, p)) {\n            heap_replace_root(hd, hi, hl, k, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        }\n    }\n    local_n[tid] = hn;\n}\n// merge\nfloat *g_d = malloc(k*sizeof(float));\nlong *g_i = malloc(k*sizeof(long));\nchar *g_l = malloc(k*sizeof(char));\nint gn=0;\nfor (int t=0; t<nthreads; ++t) {\n    int cnt = local_n[t];\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)t*k;\n    long *hi = local_idx + (size_t)t*k;\n    char *hl = local_lab + (size_t)t*k;\n    for (int j=0; j<cnt; ++j) {\n        // push hd[j], hi[j], hl[j] into global heap\n        if (gn < k) heap_push(g_d,g_i,g_l,&gn,hd[j],hi[j],hl[j]);\n        else if (is_worse(g_d[0],g_i[0],hd[j],hi[j])) heap_replace_root(...);\n    }\n}\n```\nBut note: The local heaps are max-heaps; iterating over their array in arbitrary order and pushing into global heap with same comparator yields correct top-k because we consider all candidates. However, if a local heap has more than k? It never exceeds k (heap size capped at k). So total candidates <= nthreads*k.\n\nPotential issue: The `#pragma omp for schedule(static)` inside parallel region will distribute iterations; each thread's p values are contiguous and increasing because static schedule assigns blocks in order. Yes, with default chunking, OpenMP divides iteration space into contiguous chunks of size ceil(N/num_threads) assigned to threads in order. So within a thread, p increases.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: The local heap tie-breaking uses original index p; since p increases within the thread's chunk, it matches sequential behavior for that subset. Good.\n\nNow we must ensure no data race on xs, ys, plabels reads (read-only) and tx/ty. Fine.\n\nThis transformation eliminates dists array allocation and its memory traffic, and parallelizes selection. It also removes one pass over total to compute distances separately; now distance computed once per point inside selection loop. This should be faster.\n\nNeed ensure `heap_push` and `heap_replace_root` are thread-safe? They operate on local arrays only, so yes.\n\nPotential issue: The OpenMP parallel region includes the for loop with heap operations that have branches; compiler may not vectorize due to dependencies within each thread's heap updates (sequential per point). But we already had distance SIMD in separate loop. Now we lose explicit SIMD over distances because inside selection loop there are data-dependent branches and heap modifications, preventing auto-vectorization of the whole loop. However the distance computation itself is simple; compiler might still vectorize if it can prove no dependencies? It cannot due to heap updates. So we may lose SIMD for distance calculation, which was a major speedup (parent 0.031s). That could hurt more than help.\n\nAlternative: Keep separate parallel distance loop with SIMD as before, then parallelize selection using local heaps reading from dists array. This preserves SIMD and adds parallel selection. The cost is storing/reading dists but that's already done in parent; we just add parallel selection instead of sequential heap over all points. That might improve if selection was bottleneck without losing distance SIMD.\n\nLet's compare: Parent does:\n1. Build xs,ys.\n2. Parallel simd compute dists (vectorized).\n3. Sequential heap select from dists.\n\nOur proposed change could be:\n- Keep step 2 as is (parallel simd with aligned arrays).\n- Replace sequential selection loop with parallel local heaps over chunks of p reading dists[p] and plabels[p]. This avoids recomputing distances, keeps SIMD for distance. The selection loop now reads dists array sequentially per thread; no vectorization but that's okay because heap ops dominate.\n\nThis seems safer: we don't lose the proven speedup from distance SIMD. We just parallelize selection using local heaps and merge. This is a legal transformation preserving exact semantics as argued.\n\nLet's implement this variant:\n- Keep dists allocation and computation exactly as current.\n- After dists computed, instead of single heap over all p, use OpenMP parallel with per-thread heaps processing contiguous chunks (using `#pragma omp for schedule(static)`), then merge.\n\nNeed ensure the local heap arrays are allocated before parallel. Use max threads.\n\nPotential issue: The number of threads used in distance loop may differ from selection loop if OMP_NUM_THREADS changes? It's same environment; but we can use same `omp_get_max_threads()` and rely on default team size. To be safe, inside parallel region get actual num_threads and allocate local arrays dynamically per thread using a global array of pointers sized by max threads. But simpler: allocate for max_threads.\n\nLet's write code carefully.\n\nAlso need include <omp.h>? The current knn.c doesn't include omp.h but uses OpenMP pragmas; compiler may implicitly know? Usually you should include <omp.h> to use omp_get_max_threads etc. We can add `#include <omp.h>` at top of knn.c (or in header). That's allowed.\n\nAlso need ensure no warnings treated as errors: The previous build failure was due to maybe-uninitialized most_frequent; current code initializes labels[0] and if k>0? If k could be 0, then labels array size 0 malloc(0) may return non-null but accessing labels[0] is UB. Problem likely k>=1. But compiler might warn about most_frequent uninitialized if k==0 path? In current code, `char most_frequent = labels[0];` after qsort; if k=0 then malloc(0) and labels[0] invalid; but problem guarantees k>0 probably. The previous failure was in a different version where maybe they had loop without initialization. Current code seems okay.\n\nWe'll add omp.h include.\n\nNow implement merge carefully:\n- After parallel region, we have local_n[t], local_d/t/l arrays.\n- We need to select global top-k from all candidates. Since total candidates <= nthreads*k, and k may be large (e.g., if k > total/nthreads? Actually each thread's heap size capped at k; if total < k*nthreads, some heaps have less). The merge loop pushes each candidate into a global max-heap of capacity k. This is O(nthreads*k log k) which is small.\n\nBut we must ensure that the union candidates indeed contain all global top-k. Proof: For any point p not in its chunk's local heap, there are at least k points in same chunk with better (distance,index). Those k points are also globally better than p, so p cannot be among global top-k. Thus every global top-k point is in some local heap. Good.\n\nNow tie-breaking: The merge uses same comparator; pushing all candidates into a max-heap yields correct set regardless of order because the comparator defines total order and we maintain best k according to it. So final heap contents match sequential algorithm's selected points (set, not necessarily order). Then labels array is copied from heap_lab in arbitrary heap order then qsort by label; that matches original behavior because original also copies heap_lab unsorted then sorts labels. The set of labels is same, so frequency counts and tie-breaking among groups depend only on multiset of labels, not their order before sorting. So output correct.\n\nPotential issue: If two points have identical distance and index? Impossible since indices unique. Good.\n\nNow implement in code.\n\nWe need modify src/knn.c only; header unchanged maybe add omp.h include there or in c file. We'll add `#include <omp.h>` at top of knn.c after \"knn.h\".\n\nLet's write the new selection section replacing from `float *heap_d = ...` through before labels allocation.\n\nCurrent code:\n```\n    float *heap_d = (float *)malloc(k * sizeof(float));\n    long *heap_idx = (long *)malloc(k * sizeof(long));\n    char *heap_lab = (char *)malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    int heap_n = 0;\n\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float d = dists[p];\n        if (heap_n < k) {\n            heap_push(heap_d, heap_idx, heap_lab, &heap_n,\n                      d, p, plabels[p]);\n        } else if (is_worse(heap_d[0], heap_idx[0], d, p)) {\n            heap_replace_root(heap_d, heap_idx, heap_lab, k,\n                              d, p, plabels[p]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    char *labels = ...\n```\n\nReplace with parallel version.\n\nNeed allocate local arrays:\n```\n    int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    if (max_threads < 1) max_threads = 1;\n    size_t per = (size_t)k; // k elements per thread\n    float *local_d = (float *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(float));\n    long *local_i = (long *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(long));\n    char *local_l = (char *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(char));\n    int *local_n = (int *)calloc(max_threads, sizeof(int));\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)tid * per;\n    long *hi = local_i + (size_t)tid * per;\n    char *hl = local_l + (size_t)tid * per;\n    int hn = 0;\n\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float d = dists[p];\n        if (hn < k) {\n            heap_push(hd, hi, hl, &hn, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        } else if (is_worse(hd[0], hi[0], d, p)) {\n            heap_replace_root(hd, hi, hl, k, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        }\n    }\n    local_n[tid] = hn;\n}\n```\nBut note: `#pragma omp for` inside parallel region will distribute iterations; each thread's tid is fixed. However the variable `hn` is private to each thread (declared inside). Good.\n\nAfter parallel:\n```\n    float *heap_d = (float *)malloc(k * sizeof(float));\n    long *heap_idx = (long *)malloc(k * sizeof(long));\n    char *heap_lab = (char *)malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    int heap_n = 0;\n\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; ++t) {\n        int cnt = local_n[t];\n        float *hd = local_d + (size_t)t * per;\n        long *hi = local_i + (size_t)t * per;\n        char *hl = local_l + (size_t)t * per;\n        for (int j = 0; j < cnt; ++j) {\n            if (heap_n < k) {\n                heap_push(heap_d, heap_idx, heap_lab, &heap_n,\n                          hd[j], hi[j], hl[j]);\n            } else if (is_worse(heap_d[0], heap_idx[0], hd[j], hi[j])) {\n                heap_replace_root(heap_d, heap_idx, heap_lab, k,\n                                  hd[j], hi[j], hl[j]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(local_d); free(local_i); free(local_l); free(local_n);\n```\nThen continue with labels as before.\n\nPotential issue: If max_threads > actual threads used in parallel region (e.g., OMP_NUM_THREADS set lower than omp_get_max_threads? Actually omp_get_max_threads returns maximum number of threads available for parallel regions, which is usually min(OMP_NUM_THREADS, cores). The `#pragma omp parallel` will use that many by default. So max_threads should equal actual thread count unless dynamic adjustment; but if OpenMP uses fewer due to nested or something, some local_n[t] remain 0 and merge skips them. That's fine.\n\nBut there is a subtle bug: The `#pragma omp for schedule(static)` inside parallel region will use the current team size (which may be less than max_threads). If actual threads < max_threads, then only tids 0..actual-1 are used; local_n for higher remain 0. Fine.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that `local_d` etc. are allocated with size max_threads * k; if k is large and max_threads maybe 4, okay.\n\nPotential performance: The merge loop over all candidates (max_threads*k) may be small relative to total. Good.\n\nNow check correctness for edge cases:\n- If total == 0? Problem likely has at least one point. But if total=0, the parallel for does nothing; local_n all 0; heap_n remains 0; labels array filled with -1; qsort on k elements of -1; most_frequent = -1 (char) and output that char? Original sequential would also have heap_n=0 and labels all -1, so same. But accessing dists[0] not done.\n- If k > total: Each thread's local heap will contain at most its chunk size (<k), merge pushes all candidates into global heap; since total < k, heap_n = total after merging (because each candidate pushed when heap_n<k). Then labels array has first total real labels and rest -1. Same as original.\n- If k == 0: malloc(0) may return non-null but loops with hn<k false always; local heaps empty; merge none; heap_d etc size 0; then labels = malloc(0); qsort(labels,0,...) okay? Then `char most_frequent = labels[0];` invalid. But original also would do that if k=0. Problem likely k>0.\n\nNow need ensure no compiler warnings about uninitialized due to parallel? Should be fine.\n\nAlso we should consider using `#pragma omp parallel for simd` on distance loop remains; but now selection uses OpenMP too, nested parallelism not an issue because sequential after first parallel region.\n\nPotential improvement: Could combine the two parallel regions into one to avoid thread creation overhead twice. But parent already has one parallel region for distance; adding another may add small overhead (thread fork/join). However total runtime 0.031s, thread creation maybe ~microseconds; could be okay. To reduce overhead, we could fuse: compute distances and update local heaps in same loop as described earlier, but that loses SIMD vectorization due to heap branches. Alternatively use `#pragma omp parallel` once and inside do both loops with barriers? But distance loop is simd; selection loop has dependencies per thread. Could have:\n```\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    #pragma omp for simd schedule(static) aligned(...)\n    for (long p=0; p<total; ++p) { dists[p]=...; }\n    // implicit barrier at end of for? Actually `#pragma omp for` has implicit barrier unless nowait.\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p=0; p<total; ++p) { update local heap using dists[p]; }\n}\n```\nThis uses one parallel region with two worksharing loops, avoiding fork/join twice. But the first loop is simd and second not; still fine. However we need local heaps allocated before parallel (outside). This might be slightly faster.\n\nBut note: The `#pragma omp for simd` inside a parallel region will distribute iterations among threads with SIMD within each thread's chunk? Actually OpenMP combined worksharing-simd means the loop is distributed across threads and each thread vectorizes its portion. That's what current code does (parallel for simd). If we put it inside an outer `#pragma omp parallel`, we need to use `#pragma omp for simd` not `parallel for simd`. The semantics similar.\n\nLet's consider fusing:\n```\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    #pragma omp for simd schedule(static) aligned(xs, ys, dists: 64)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float dx = xs[p] - tx;\n        float dy = ys[p] - ty;\n        dists[p] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\n    }\n    // implicit barrier at end of worksharing loop? Actually `#pragma omp for` has an implicit barrier at the end unless nowait is specified. So all threads finish distance before selection.\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)tid*per; ...\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p=0; p<(long)total; ++p) {\n        // update heap\n    }\n}\n```\nThis avoids two parallel region creations. But note: The first `#pragma omp for simd` has an implicit barrier at end, so selection starts after all distances computed. Good.\n\nHowever, the local heap arrays must be allocated before this combined parallel region. We can allocate them outside using max_threads as before.\n\nThis is a good optimization: one fork/join instead of two. Let's implement that.\n\nNeed ensure `#pragma omp for simd` inside parallel works with aligned clause; yes syntax: `#pragma omp for simd schedule(static) aligned(xs, ys, dists: 64)`.\n\nAlso need to declare tid and local pointers after first loop but before second. Since each thread executes both loops in same team.\n\nPotential issue: The variable `tid` is private per thread; fine.\n\nLet's write the new code section from after plabels built through selection.\n\nCurrent:\n```\n    float *dists = NULL;\n    if (posix_memalign((void **)&dists, 64, total * sizeof(float)) != 0) {\n        dists = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\n    }\n    float tx = to_evaluate.x;\n    float ty = to_evaluate.y;\n\n#pragma omp parallel for simd schedule(static) aligned(xs, ys, dists: 64)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float dx = xs[p] - tx;\n        float dy = ys[p] - ty;\n        dists[p] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\n    }\n\n    float *heap_d = ...\n```\n\nReplace with:\n```\n    float *dists = NULL;\n    if (posix_memalign((void **)&dists, 64, total * sizeof(float)) != 0) {\n        dists = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\n    }\n    float tx = to_evaluate.x;\n    float ty = to_evaluate.y;\n\n    int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    if (max_threads < 1) max_threads = 1;\n    size_t per = (size_t)k;\n    float *local_d = (float *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(float));\n    long *local_i = (long *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(long));\n    char *local_l = (char *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(char));\n    int *local_n = (int *)calloc(max_threads, sizeof(int));\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    #pragma omp for simd schedule(static) aligned(xs, ys, dists: 64)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float dx = xs[p] - tx;\n        float dy = ys[p] - ty;\n        dists[p] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\n    }\n\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)tid * per;\n    long *hi = local_i + (size_t)tid * per;\n    char *hl = local_l + (size_t)tid * per;\n    int hn = 0;\n\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float d = dists[p];\n        if (hn < k) {\n            heap_push(hd, hi, hl, &hn, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        } else if (is_worse(hd[0], hi[0], d, p)) {\n            heap_replace_root(hd, hi, hl, k, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        }\n    }\n    local_n[tid] = hn;\n}\n\n    float *heap_d = ...\n```\nBut note: The `#pragma omp for simd` inside parallel has an implicit barrier at its end? According to OpenMP spec, a worksharing loop without nowait has an implicit barrier at the end. So yes.\n\nHowever, there is a subtle issue: The variable `tid` and local pointers are declared after first loop but before second; each thread executes them in order. Good.\n\nBut we must ensure that `local_n[tid] = hn;` happens after the second for loop; it's inside parallel region after worksharing loop, so all threads execute it (unless there is a barrier? The second `#pragma omp for` also has implicit barrier at end, then assignment). Fine.\n\nPotential issue: If total=0, both loops no iterations; hn remains 0; local_n set to 0. Good.\n\nNow after parallel region, merge as before.\n\nNeed include <omp.h> in knn.c.\n\nAlso need ensure `calloc` available from stdlib (already included via header). Yes.\n\nPotential compiler warning: `per = k` where k int; if k=0 then per=0 and malloc(0) may return non-null but loops with hn<k false. Fine.\n\nNow let's consider whether this transformation is legal regarding SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies. It preserves exact semantics as argued. The role B goal \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\" - we are adding parallel selection while keeping SIMD distance, respecting dependencies by using per-thread heaps with contiguous chunks.\n\nWe should also consider if `#pragma omp for simd` inside a parallel region is allowed with aligned clause; yes.\n\nPotential performance: The second loop (selection) reads dists and plabels sequentially per thread. It may have poor cache behavior due to heap operations but okay.\n\nCould we further optimize by using `schedule(static, chunk)`? Default static gives contiguous chunks of roughly equal size. Good for locality.\n\nNow let's write the full modified knn.c content. We'll include omp.h at top after \"knn.h\". Also maybe add restrict to helper functions? Not necessary but could help; however we should avoid changing too much beyond our hypothesis. The parent already has alignment and simd; our change is parallel selection.\n\nLet's produce final file content exactly.\n\nWe need ensure no other changes: keep main, parsing etc unchanged.\n\nLet's write the new knn.c:\n\n```\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n\nstatic int is_worse(float da, long ia, float db, long ib) {\n    return (da > db) || (da == db && ia > ib);\n}\n\nstatic void swap3(float *hd, long *hi, char *hl, int a, int b) {\n    float td = hd[a]; hd[a] = hd[b]; hd[b] = td;\n    long ti = hi[a]; hi[a] = hi[b]; hi[b] = ti;\n    char tc = hl[a]; hl[a] = hl[b]; hl[b] = tc;\n}\n\nstatic void heap_push(float *hd, long *hi, char *hl, int *n,\n                      float d, long idx, char label) {\n    int i = (*n)++;\n    hd[i] = d; hi[i] = idx; hl[i] = label;\n\n    while (i > 0) {\n        int parent = (i - 1) / 2;\n        if (is_worse(hd[i], hi[i], hd[parent], hi[parent])) {\n            swap3(hd, hi, hl, i, parent);\n            i = parent;\n        } else {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstatic void heap_replace_root(float *hd, long *hi, char *hl, int n,\n                              float d, long idx, char label) {\n    hd[0] = d; hi[0] = idx; hl[0] = label;\n    int i = 0;\n\n    for (;;) {\n        int left = 2 * i + 1;\n        if (left >= n) break;\n        int right = left + 1;\n        int worst = i;\n\n        if (is_worse(hd[left], hi[left], hd[worst], hi[worst])) {\n            worst = left;\n        }\n        if (right < n && is_worse(hd[right], hi[right], hd[worst], hi[worst])) {\n            worst = right;\n        }\n\n        if (worst == i) break;\n        swap3(hd, hi, hl, i, worst);\n        i = worst;\n    }\n}\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    size_t total = 0;\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; ++gi) {\n        total += (size_t)groups[gi].length;\n    }\n\n    float *xs = NULL;\n    if (posix_memalign((void **)&xs, 64, total * sizeof(float)) != 0) {\n        xs = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\n    }\n    float *ys = NULL;\n    if (posix_memalign((void **)&ys, 64, total * sizeof(float)) != 0) {\n        ys = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\n    }\n    char *plabels = (char *)malloc(total * sizeof(char));\n\n    size_t pos = 0;\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; ++gi) {\n        Group g = groups[gi];\n        char lab = g.label;\n        for (int j = 0; j < g.length; ++j) {\n            xs[pos] = g.points[j].x;\n            ys[pos] = g.points[j].y;\n            plabels[pos] = lab;\n            ++pos;\n        }\n    }\n\n    float *dists = NULL;\n    if (posix_memalign((void **)&dists, 64, total * sizeof(float)) != 0) {\n        dists = malloc(total * sizeof(float));\n    }\n    float tx = to_evaluate.x;\n    float ty = to_evaluate.y;\n\n    int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    if (max_threads < 1) max_threads = 1;\n    size_t per = (size_t)k;\n    float *local_d = (float *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(float));\n    long *local_i = (long *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(long));\n    char *local_l = (char *)malloc((size_t)max_threads * per * sizeof(char));\n    int *local_n = (int *)calloc(max_threads, sizeof(int));\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    #pragma omp for simd schedule(static) aligned(xs, ys, dists: 64)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float dx = xs[p] - tx;\n        float dy = ys[p] - ty;\n        dists[p] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\n    }\n\n    int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n    float *hd = local_d + (size_t)tid * per;\n    long *hi = local_i + (size_t)tid * per;\n    char *hl = local_l + (size_t)tid * per;\n    int hn = 0;\n\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n    for (long p = 0; p < (long)total; ++p) {\n        float d = dists[p];\n        if (hn < k) {\n            heap_push(hd, hi, hl, &hn, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        } else if (is_worse(hd[0], hi[0], d, p)) {\n            heap_replace_root(hd, hi, hl, k, d, p, plabels[p]);\n        }\n    }\n    local_n[tid] = hn;\n}\n\n    float *heap_d = (float *)malloc(k * sizeof(float));\n    long *heap_idx = (long *)malloc(k * sizeof(long));\n    char *heap_lab = (char *)malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    int heap_n = 0;\n\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; ++t) {\n        int cnt = local_n[t];\n        float *hd = local_d + (size_t)t * per;\n        long *hi = local_i + (size_t)t * per;\n        char *hl = local_l + (size_t)t * per;\n        for (int j = 0; j < cnt; ++j) {\n            if (heap_n < k) {\n                heap_push(heap_d, heap_idx, heap_lab, &heap_n,\n                          hd[j], hi[j], hl[j]);\n            } else if (is_worse(heap_d[0], heap_idx[0], hd[j], hi[j])) {\n                heap_replace_root(heap_d, heap_idx, heap_lab, k,\n                                  hd[j], hi[j], hl[j]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(local_d); free(local_i); free(local_l); free(local_n);\n\n    char *labels = (char *)malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {\n        if (i < heap_n) labels[i] = heap_lab[i];\n        else labels[i] = -1;\n    }\n\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; ++i) {\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            ++current_frequency;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(xs); free(ys); free(plabels); free(dists);\n    free(heap_d); free(heap_idx); free(heap_lab); free(labels);\n\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\n\n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; ++i) {\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\n    }\n\n    int k = parse_k();\n\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\n\n    printf(\"%c\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nNeed check if `#pragma omp for simd` inside parallel region with aligned clause is accepted by GCC on ARM64. Should be.\n\nPotential issue: The first loop's `schedule(static)` and second loop's `schedule(static)` may assign different chunks to threads? They both use static schedule without chunk, so each thread gets contiguous blocks of roughly equal size; but the boundaries might differ because total same and num_threads same. However that doesn't matter for correctness: each point processed exactly once in first loop (by some thread) and exactly once in second loop (possibly by different thread). The local heap is per-thread, so a given p may be assigned to thread A in distance computation and thread B in selection; but dists array is shared and written/read after barrier. That's fine.\n\nBut wait: In the first `#pragma omp for simd`, each iteration writes dists[p]. After implicit barrier at end of worksharing loop, all threads have completed writing all dists. Then second loop reads them; no race because barrier ensures completion. Good.\n\nNow consider if `max_threads` is greater than actual team size due to dynamic adjustment? The parallel region will use a number of threads determined by OMP_NUM_THREADS and possibly dynamic. If it uses fewer, some local_n entries remain 0; merge skips them. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: We allocate local arrays with `per = k` elements per thread. But if actual team size > max_threads? Impossible because omp_get_max_threads returns upper bound for parallel regions without num_threads clause. So okay.\n\nNow let's think about correctness proof more formally to include in notes/hypothesis.\n\nWe should also consider whether the merge step could produce a different set due to local heaps containing points that are not globally top-k but still within union; merging selects best k from union, which equals global top-k as argued. Good.\n\nPotential edge: If two points have same distance and index? No.\n\nNow we need output JSON with files content. Only changed file is src/knn.c (added omp.h include and modified selection). Header unchanged. We'll provide full content of knn.c only; maybe also header if unchanged but not necessary? The instructions say \"Return COMPLETE contents of changed files\". So only knn.c.\n\nWe need set sources_kept: [] because integration_source_ids empty.\n\nTransformations list: describe changes, e.g., [\"Parallelize top-k selection with per-thread heaps over contiguous 